<article class="env-lemma" id="01ZO">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01ZO">Lemma <span data-tag="01ZO">32.8.3</span>.</a> Notation and assumptions as in Situation <a href="/tag/081D" data-tag="081D">32.8.1</a>. If </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$f$ is a finite morphism, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$f_0$ is locally of finite type, 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> then there exists an $i \geq 0$ such that $f_ i$ is finite. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      A finite morphism is affine, see Morphisms, Definition <a href="/tag/01WH" data-tag="01WH">29.45.1</a>. Hence by Lemma <a href="/tag/01ZN" data-tag="01ZN">32.8.2</a> above after increasing $0$ we may assume that $f_0$ is affine. By writing $Y_0$ as a finite union of affines we reduce to proving the result when $X_0$ and $Y_0$ are affine and map into a common affine $W \subset S_0$. The corresponding algebra statement follows from Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/07RG" data-tag="07RG">10.168.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>